; Registry over TLS, with certificate verification ON.
;
; These two lines restate npm's own defaults. They are written out rather than left
; implicit because this file previously carried the opposite of both, for nearly three
; years, and its absence would not record why.
;
; From 2023-11-20 (84da5c2, "Vite was installed") until 2026-09-08 this file read:
;
;     registry=http://registry.npmjs.org/
;     strict-ssl=false
;
; Two consequences, both measured before the change:
;
;   1. `npm audit` could not run at all. The registry answers a plaintext audit POST with
;      `426 Upgrade Required`, so every audit — locally and in CI — died. The frontend has
;      therefore never produced a vulnerability count. With TLS restored it reports 52
;      advisories (3 critical / 30 high, all severities incl. dev).
;
;   2. `strict-ssl=false` disabled certificate verification for every npm metadata request.
;      Installs themselves stayed integrity-checked — package-lock.json v3 pins 1360 https
;      tarball URLs and zero http ones — so this was an exposure on dependency RESOLUTION,
;      not on the pinned artifacts. Narrower than it looks, and still not a setting to keep.
;
; Verified after the change: `npm ci` exits 0 and `npm audit --json` returns a populated
; metadata.vulnerabilities block.
;
; ⛔ Do not set `strict-ssl=false` to work around a TLS error. It converts a loud failure
; into a silent trust hole, which is exactly how it survived here undetected.
registry=https://registry.npmjs.org/
strict-ssl=true
